Severe Vasculogenic Erectile Dysfunction (Untreated Metabolic/Vascular Disease)
Genitourinary Renal & Mens Health
Progressive loss of erectile rigidity, Absent morning/nocturnal erections, Diminished peripheral pulses, Abnormal penile Doppler

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GRM-041
Reason for Visit: Acute chest tightness and severe lower extremity cramping precipitating an anxiety crisis following a completely failed attempt at sexual intercourse.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and partner
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient experiencing acute exertional chest pain and severe calf claudication during sexual activity, culminating in sudden erectile failure and severe distress. Urgent after-hours assessment was strictly mandated to rule out an acute coronary syndrome or critical limb ischaemia. Delaying evaluation until the next available in-hours period posed a severe risk of an unrecognized acute myocardial infarction.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My chest got incredibly tight, my calves started burning, and I completely lost my erection and could not get it back.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a progressive, insidious onset of an inability to achieve or maintain a rigid erection over the past year. They note an absolute lack of early morning or spontaneous nocturnal erections and a failure to achieve full rigidity even with adequate sexual stimulation or masturbation. Tonight, during an attempt at intercourse, they experienced exertional angina pectoris and intermittent claudication presenting as reproducible calf pain, which forced them to stop. The patient has poorly controlled hypertension and central abdominal obesity indicative of the metabolic syndrome.
Functional Status: Currently resting in a chair, distressed and fearful of a heart attack.
Social/Safety Concerns: High cardiovascular risk; severe relationship strain secondary to undiagnosed erectile dysfunction.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 165/95 | HR: 98 | Temp: 36.6C | RR: 18 | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 108 Kg
General: Diaphoretic, visibly anxious, central adiposity present, recovering from acute exertional symptoms.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Diminished distal peripheral pulses (dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial) bilaterally. A carotid bruit is audible on the left side. Heart sounds are regular with a soft systolic murmur. Genitourinary: Examination deferred as unnecessary for the acute cardiovascular presentation. Expected investigation results will demonstrate diminished peak systolic velocity within the cavernosal arteries during a pharmacologically stimulated penile Doppler ultrasound, indicating severe arterial insufficiency.
Environmental Observations: High-sodium snack wrappers and physical inactivity cues present in the living area.
Medication Reconciliation: Non-compliant with prescribed perindopril for the past three months.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Vasculogenic Erectile Dysfunction (Untreated Metabolic/Vascular Disease) - Status: Exacerbated (Anginal Equivalent)
Differentials:

Psychogenic Erectile Dysfunction - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absolute lack of early morning or spontaneous nocturnal erections, combined with the objective presence of severe systemic vascular disease.

Male Hypogonadism - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary solitary cause given the prominent claudication, bruits, and diminished pulses pointing explicitly to severe arterial inflow obstruction.

Acute Myocardial Infarction - Ruled out/unlikely as the chest tightness completely resolved with five minutes of rest and normal ECG findings were obtained on-site, consistent with stable angina rather than acute infarction.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibits severe vasculogenic erectile dysfunction acting as a sentinel marker for widespread, untreated atherosclerotic disease. The acute presentation of exertional chest pain and claudication during sexual activity represents a significant ischemic threshold. Urgent after-hours clinical evaluation, including a 12-lead ECG and vascular examination, was essential to rule out an active myocardial infarction and stabilize the patient. Deferring this assessment would have exposed the patient to the immediate, life-threatening risk of a major adverse cardiovascular event.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Aspirin 300mg chewed stat. Reinstated oral Perindopril immediately to manage the severe hypertension. Arranged an urgent, fast-tracked cardiology referral for a formal exercise stress test and echocardiogram.
Positive Behavioral Change: To initiate immediate cardiovascular risk reduction, the patient is instructed to complete a ten-minute slow walk every morning before breakfast. By linking the walk to an established daily routine, the patient reliably executes the behavior. Experiencing a gradual reduction in resting blood pressure over two weeks will provide the positive reinforcement needed to sustain and lengthen the daily exercise habit.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None required immediately.
DME/Safety (Optional): Home blood pressure monitor recommended.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP review in 48 hours to assess blood pressure control and confirm the cardiology appointment.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department via ambulance is required if the chest pain returns at rest, radiates to the jaw or arm, or is accompanied by severe shortness of breath or dizziness.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The erectile dysfunction is a symptom of severe blockages in your blood vessels; formal heart investigations are legally and medically required before any erectile medications can be safely prescribed.
Clinical Course: The chest pain resolved with rest. Cardiovascular fitness and erectile function may improve slowly with aggressive risk factor management and weight loss.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If you experience sudden, crushing chest pain while resting, or if you feel faint and sweaty, you must call an ambulance immediately. Do not attempt to drive.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The arteries supplying blood to your penis are very small and are often the first to become blocked by high blood pressure and cholesterol. Tonight, the physical exertion of sex also starved your heart and leg muscles of oxygen, causing the chest and calf pain. We must prioritize investigating your heart to prevent a fatal heart attack before we address the erectile dysfunction.
Actionable Steps: Take the aspirin and blood pressure medication exactly as prescribed. Do not attempt any vigorous physical or sexual activity until cleared by the cardiologist.

